Question 1 — Deduplication using Window Functions
Hard
Give the candidate this DataFrame:
customer_id	name	amount	updated_at
101	Ravi	500	2026-10-25 10:00
101	Ravi	800	2026-10-26 11:00
102	Kumar	300	2026-10-24 09:00
102	Kumar	400	2026-10-26 12:00
103	Priya	900	2026-10-25 15:00

Ask him to write code to:
1. Retain only the latest record for each customer.
2. Return all columns.
3. Handle ties in updated_at deterministically if a suitable tie-breaker is available.
Expected output:
customer_id	name	amount	updated_at
101	Ravi	800	2026-10-26 11:00
102	Kumar	400	2026-10-26 12:00
103	Priya	900	2026-10-25 15:00

Expected solution:
from pyspark.sql import functions as Ffrom pyspark.sql.window import Windowwindow_spec = (    Window.partitionBy("customer_id")    .orderBy(F.col("updated_at").desc()))result = (    df.withColumn("rn", F.row_number().over(window_spec))      .filter(F.col("rn") == 1)      .drop("rn"))result.show()



Follow-up: Why can't we simply use dropDuplicates(["customer_id"])?

Expected: It does not guarantee that the latest record is retained.

